//! # Core concepts
//!
//! Hew combines value-oriented programming with actors and structured
//! concurrency. This page gives the short version of the ideas that shape most
//! Hew programs and points to the guide for each one.
//!
//! ```hew
//! actor Counter {
//!     var value: i64 = 0;
//!     receive fn add(amount: i64) { value += amount; }
//!     receive fn read() -> i64 { value }
//! }
//!
//! fn main() {
//!     let counter = spawn Counter;
//!     counter.add(2).expect("counter accepts updates");
//!     println(counter.read().expect("counter replies"));
//!     stop(counter);
//!     stopped(counter);
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! The caller holds an actor reference, not direct access to `Counter`'s
//! fields. Calls deliver messages, and the actor handles one message at a time.
//! The completion result says whether the call was handled successfully.
//!
//! ## Values, bindings, and ownership
//!
//! A value has one or more bindings, such as a local name, a record field, or
//! a collection element. `let` prevents assignment through a binding; `var`
//! permits it. Neither word means “shared” or “owned.”
//!
//! Ownership answers a different question: which value is responsible for an
//! owner such as a file, stream, task, or captured closure state? Ordinary data
//! can produce an independent value when reused. An affine owner transfers
//! instead; after transfer, the previous place cannot use it. A function marks
//! that transfer with `consume`. See [ownership and sending](language.ownership.html).
//!
//! ## Resources and cleanup
//!
//! A resource is affine: it has at most one owner, and leaving scope safely
//! invokes its consuming `close` method. Explicitly closing it ends ownership
//! earlier. A linear value is stricter: every path must explicitly consume it,
//! because there is no safe automatic action. See
//! [resources and linear values](language.resources.html).
//!
//! ## Actors, messages, and isolation
//!
//! An actor owns private state and processes one handler at a time. Other code
//! interacts with it through a typed actor reference. Message data must satisfy
//! [Send](language.ownership.html#send): ordinary data arrives as an
//! independent snapshot, actor references preserve identity, and admitted
//! affine owners transfer. See [actors](language.actors.html) for completion
//! calls, submissions, mailbox pressure, periodic handlers, and interceptors.
//!
//! ## Tasks and structured concurrency
//!
//! A task is concurrent work owned by a lexical `scope`. The scope does not
//! finish until its children and their cleanup settle. `fork` starts a task;
//! `await`, `race`, and `select` coordinate results. Actors have independent
//! identities and lifetimes, while tasks remain bounded by their parent scope.
//! See [structured concurrency](language.concurrency.html).
//!
//! ## Failure and supervision
//!
//! Expected failure travels through `Result` and a declared `fails E` edge.
//! Panics, checked traps, and failed actor turns are faults. A supervisor owns
//! child roles and applies restart or escalation policy after faults; it does
//! not replace ordinary error handling. See [functions and errors](language.functions.html)
//! and [supervision](language.supervision.html).
//!
//! ## State machines, wire data, and targets
//!
//! A `machine` is an ordinary value whose generated `step` method applies
//! exhaustive transition rules and returns typed outputs. It has no mailbox or
//! thread of its own. See [state machines](language.machines.html).
//!
//! `#[wire]` gives records, enums, and machines stable serialized identities
//! for service and remote-actor boundaries. See [wire data](language.wire.html).
//! Native, WASI, and browser runtimes admit different host capabilities; check
//! [target capabilities](language.targets.html) before choosing a deployment
//! target.
//!
//! ## Further reading
//!
//! The [concepts path on hew.sh](https://hew.sh/docs/concepts) introduces these
//! ideas in a concept-led, task-oriented sequence. Continue with its guides to
//! [ownership](https://hew.sh/docs/concepts/ownership),
//! [message passing](https://hew.sh/docs/concepts/message-passing),
//! [actor lifecycle](https://hew.sh/docs/concepts/actor-lifecycle), and
//! [structured concurrency](https://hew.sh/docs/concepts/structured-concurrency).
//! The reference pages above remain the source for Hew-specific syntax and
//! behavior.
//!
//! External material can provide background on the broader ideas, but its
//! syntax and lifetime rules do not replace Hew's language reference.
//!
//! - The [Rust ownership chapter](https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch04-00-understanding-ownership.html)
//!   develops ownership and borrowing as a general systems-programming model.
//!   Rust's references and lifetime syntax are not Hew syntax.
//! - Oracle's [structured concurrency overview](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/core/structured-concurrency.html)
//!   explains why child work is grouped under a scope. Its Java API is
//!   different from Hew's `scope`, `fork`, and cancellation rules.
//! - The [Erlang/OTP design principles](https://www.erlang.org/doc/system/design_principles.html)
//!   introduce processes and supervision trees. Hew adopts related fault
//!   isolation ideas with its own typed messages, ownership, and restart rules.
